WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.116 --> 00:00:03.777
After decades of rising debt,

00:00:03.857 --> 00:00:04.996
dwindling population,

00:00:05.016 --> 00:00:06.518
and increased racial tensions,

00:00:06.718 --> 00:00:12.001
the birthplace of Motown and the American auto industry is mounting a comeback.

00:00:12.141 --> 00:00:14.041
$19 billion in debt,

00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:16.323
unemployment rate of about 14%,

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:17.983
about a third of the population.

00:00:18.423 --> 00:00:20.446
has moved out over the last several decades.

00:00:20.585 --> 00:00:22.887
It's great to see these stories about Detroit making a comeback.

00:00:22.948 --> 00:00:24.109
And if you spend any time there,

00:00:24.129 --> 00:00:26.150
people are so invested in making it happen.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.112
You have some gentrifying going on.

00:00:29.173 --> 00:00:31.655
You have people who have been in the community for years.

00:00:31.675 --> 00:00:32.736
They're being pushed out.

00:00:32.915 --> 00:00:36.819
The new Detroit is only the new Detroit because the old Detroit is the foundation.

00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:38.221
Downtown is hopping,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.001
so is Midtown.

00:00:39.021 --> 00:00:39.161
I mean,

00:00:39.161 --> 00:00:41.903
you literally can't even find a place or an apartment here.

00:00:41.923 --> 00:00:46.546
We want to live here and we want to do what we've been doing all of our lives.

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:47.188
We struggle in it.

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:48.768
and everybody is.

00:00:48.888 --> 00:00:49.710
Everybody's struggling here.

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:50.811
Been here all my life.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:51.731
I've seen the good.

00:00:51.871 --> 00:00:52.753
Now I'm seeing the bad.

00:00:53.292 --> 00:00:54.673
Now hopefully I'll see the good again.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:55.214
Thank you,

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:55.454
Dan,

00:00:55.494 --> 00:00:56.435
for what you're doing for Detroit.

00:00:58.016 --> 00:00:59.137
Why should Detroit be saved,

00:00:59.138 --> 00:00:59.878
of all the cities?

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:01.399
Detroit doesn't need to be saved.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.121
Detroit is flourishing.

00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:23.406
You are about to witness the very exciting story of a city and its people.

00:01:26.228 --> 00:01:31.150
It will be an adventure that will open new sights in familiar surroundings.

00:01:31.671 --> 00:01:37.894
It is a story of a city seeking new horizons and a resolute contest with great challenges.

00:01:39.096 --> 00:01:40.676
That city is Detroit.

00:01:41.617 --> 00:01:41.878
Yes,

00:01:41.977 --> 00:01:44.379
Detroit is enjoying its finest hour.

00:01:44.959 --> 00:01:46.381
There is a renaissance,

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.201
a rebirth in the city.

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:50.444
There's a newness in Detroit.

00:01:54.506 --> 00:01:55.507
Whenever you hear about Detroit,

00:01:55.507 --> 00:01:56.828
you hear about two extremes.

00:01:56.828 --> 00:02:02.230
You hear that it's an abandoned city and it's an up-and-coming city.

00:02:03.548 --> 00:02:06.189
Which are both true in some regard,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:11.454
but it seems like an oversimplified version of what's really happening here.

00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:14.336
I think there's a lot of gray area in the middle,

00:02:15.056 --> 00:02:15.336
and

00:02:15.877 --> 00:02:16.676
I want to explore that.

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:22.882
I want to know what everyday Detroiters think about what's going on in their city and what their city is to them.

00:02:25.043 --> 00:02:29.045
My first stop is a place where many of those everyday Detroiters used to work,

00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:31.367
the Packard Auto Plant.

00:02:41.811 --> 00:02:44.652
Getting a good dose of post-apocalyptic lifestyle right now.

00:02:49.454 --> 00:02:52.396
Walking through what is now the world's largest abandoned factory,

00:02:53.197 --> 00:02:54.597
I felt like I was in a bombed out,

00:02:54.697 --> 00:02:55.457
forgotten city.

00:02:57.098 --> 00:02:59.159
And the Packard plant really was its own city.

00:03:00.588 --> 00:03:05.972
This massive three and a half million square foot complex once employed 36,000 people.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.056
When the factory opened in 1903,

00:03:10.057 --> 00:03:13.879
workers flocked here from all over the country for their shot at the American dream.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:21.504
Hundreds of thousands of cars were assembled here before the plant closed in 1958.

00:03:21.506 --> 00:03:25.248
What remains today is just a ghost of that great city within a city.

00:03:33.108 --> 00:03:36.769
So this hallway was the production line where they would build the cars.

00:03:37.028 --> 00:03:42.890
They would start on that end and put all the parts on the cars until it got to that end and then it would be a finished product.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.852
I'm going to say that this hallway is like three blocks long.

00:03:57.175 --> 00:04:01.475
All I had to do was take a left turn and stand in this light and then boom.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:03.336
Epic ruin porn.

00:04:04.117 --> 00:04:10.362
It's way easier to point a camera at something and say this is a problem than it is to actually fix it.

00:04:10.943 --> 00:04:17.110
I think there's a lot of people pointing at things and saying that's a problem and not a lot of people trying to fix it.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.212
I think this place in particular has a very large wow factor.

00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:25.437
So you get a lot of people coming here and photographing it and saying,

00:04:26.397 --> 00:04:27.139
this is Detroit.

00:04:29.175 --> 00:04:33.377
I feel a little nervous that we're adding to the ruin porn pile.

00:04:34.176 --> 00:04:34.978
I'm going to try not to.

00:04:36.098 --> 00:04:39.038
I don't want to just show a one-dimensional story about Detroit.

00:04:39.658 --> 00:04:42.839
It's real easy for us to come here and just fly our drone through this place and say,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:43.919
we got a story.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:49.062
But you got to think that when you come to these places that people actually live there,

00:04:49.241 --> 00:04:50.002
they grew up there,

00:04:50.101 --> 00:04:51.221
they're going to raise families there.

00:04:52.582 --> 00:04:56.323
And you can't just go there and look at their pile of garbage and say that's how they are.

00:05:01.879 --> 00:05:07.022
This place is so infamous that hundreds of people come here every day to shoot photos and to tour through the place

00:05:07.623 --> 00:05:13.408
Which eventually became a security problem because people were getting robbed for the cameras now,

00:05:13.447 --> 00:05:16.349
there's 24-7 security like we even have

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.510
security guard watching our cars today?

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.993
Majority of the people that comes to here is mostly people that stay outside of Detroit.

00:05:24.615 --> 00:05:24.834
Okay.

00:05:25.394 --> 00:05:26.755
More suburban people,

00:05:27.415 --> 00:05:27.817
you know.

00:05:28.757 --> 00:05:31.198
Very small number of locals.

00:05:31.918 --> 00:05:32.779
It's a destination.

00:05:33.895 --> 00:05:34.095
Yeah.

00:05:35.156 --> 00:05:35.676
That's what it is.

00:05:35.696 --> 00:05:36.877
Yeah.

00:05:36.937 --> 00:05:39.539
It's almost like this is Detroit's version of the Coliseum.

00:05:39.599 --> 00:05:39.959
Yes,

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:41.680
it's quite a spectacle.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.141
What's this neighborhood like now?

00:05:43.742 --> 00:05:44.182
Deserted,

00:05:44.183 --> 00:05:46.704
a lot of vacant and abandoned houses.

00:05:47.104 --> 00:05:48.205
I grew up in this neighborhood,

00:05:48.405 --> 00:05:48.705
so I

00:05:48.706 --> 00:05:50.266
I've seen the good and the bad.

00:05:50.268 --> 00:05:50.887
Right.

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:52.807
Detroit is on a climb,

00:05:52.807 --> 00:05:53.028
you know.

00:05:53.028 --> 00:05:53.508
It's on a climb?

00:05:53.927 --> 00:05:54.487
I believe so.

00:05:55.108 --> 00:05:57.028
I believe Detroit is on a climb now.

00:05:57.528 --> 00:05:59.030
Majority of this city is caring,

00:05:59.629 --> 00:06:00.050
you know.

00:06:00.689 --> 00:06:01.050
I care.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.211
And they don't report that a lot,

00:06:03.271 --> 00:06:04.891
about people caring about the city.

00:06:05.232 --> 00:06:08.312
All they hear about is the negativity of the city.

00:06:09.913 --> 00:06:10.494
Rob's right.

00:06:10.954 --> 00:06:16.235
It's easy to focus on negativity and point fingers when someone or somewhere is on the way down.

00:06:17.075 --> 00:06:19.437
But the Packard plant may be on its way back up.

00:06:20.378 --> 00:06:21.319
In 2014,

00:06:21.479 --> 00:06:24.262
a developer bought it for $405,000,

00:06:24.782 --> 00:06:27.185
with the intent of converting it to a mix of housing,

00:06:27.466 --> 00:06:27.865
retail,

00:06:27.986 --> 00:06:28.846
and public space.

00:06:29.728 --> 00:06:31.048
If all goes according to plan,

00:06:31.509 --> 00:06:33.471
this great structure will live again.

00:06:35.252 --> 00:06:36.594
It's a growing trend in this city.

00:06:37.536 --> 00:06:41.098
Abandoned property and vacant land being cleaned up and repurposed.

00:06:45.636 --> 00:06:49.257
We were just driving down the street and we saw this cleanup operation going on.

00:06:49.757 --> 00:06:51.800
We figured we'd pull over and see what's happening.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.083
This is a cleanup operation with Recovery Park Farms.

00:06:57.442 --> 00:06:57.622
Okay.

00:06:58.184 --> 00:07:00.024
And the idea is to be able to,

00:07:00.064 --> 00:07:00.365
like,

00:07:01.165 --> 00:07:08.769
take advantage of all the vacant lots in Detroit and be able to start growing on those vacant lots and servicing the product right here.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:10.569
at this historical market,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:11.831
Shane Ferry Market.

00:07:12.211 --> 00:07:12.790
That's amazing.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:13.031
Yeah,

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:13.490
yeah.

00:07:14.271 --> 00:07:16.192
I hear one of two stories with Detroit.

00:07:16.211 --> 00:07:19.492
I either hear it's an abandoned lost city or it's an up and coming city.

00:07:19.612 --> 00:07:20.153
It's true.

00:07:20.812 --> 00:07:21.213
Both of them?

00:07:21.215 --> 00:07:22.213
It's true.

00:07:22.215 --> 00:07:22.853
Both are true.

00:07:23.054 --> 00:07:23.213
Yeah.

00:07:23.473 --> 00:07:28.376
And I think this is one of the innovative ways that Detroit is quote unquote coming back.

00:07:29.643 --> 00:07:33.366
Brent told me that after the local Chrysler plant was shut down and relocated,

00:07:34.106 --> 00:07:36.828
this once vibrant neighborhood went downhill fast.

00:07:37.969 --> 00:07:38.609
Just like

00:07:38.969 --> 00:07:39.910
Flint, Michigan,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:41.351
just like Gary,

00:07:41.451 --> 00:07:42.011
Indiana,

00:07:42.432 --> 00:07:44.353
and other cities that...

00:07:44.674 --> 00:07:48.579
have large populations of people of color and poor whites working,

00:07:50.081 --> 00:07:50.982
that plant left.

00:07:51.422 --> 00:07:51.643
Okay.

00:07:52.083 --> 00:07:54.626
And this is the aftermath of the plants leaving.

00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:58.730
You also had people making money off of burning down houses for house insurance.

00:07:58.911 --> 00:07:59.052
Oh,

00:07:59.072 --> 00:07:59.292
really?

00:07:59.352 --> 00:07:59.472
Yeah.

00:07:59.551 --> 00:07:59.991
Yeah,

00:07:59.992 --> 00:08:00.352
and people,

00:08:00.572 --> 00:08:00.812
you know,

00:08:00.832 --> 00:08:01.913
were paying guys,

00:08:02.033 --> 00:08:02.433
you know,

00:08:03.574 --> 00:08:04.695
to burn down properties.

00:08:04.716 --> 00:08:05.716
If you're somebody that,

00:08:05.796 --> 00:08:06.116
you know,

00:08:06.817 --> 00:08:09.240
has been denied job offers and somebody tells you,

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:09.379
hey,

00:08:09.419 --> 00:08:09.779
throw this

00:08:10.281 --> 00:08:11.861
Molotov cocktail on this house,

